  "prediction": "**Acute Flaccid Myelitis (AFM) Outbreak: A Growing Concern**\n\nA mysterious polio-like illness, known as Acute Flaccid Myelitis (AFM), has been on the rise in the United States, with a significant increase in reported cases this year. According to the Centers for Disease Control and Prevention (CDC), there have been 32 new cases of AFM confirmed across the country through July, a sharp rise compared to last year when only seven cases had been confirmed by that month.\n\n**What is AFM?**\n\nAFM is a rare condition that affects the nervous system, specifically the spinal cord. It is characterized by muscle weakness, particularly in the arms and legs, and can lead to respiratory failure. The exact cause of AFM is still unknown, but it has been linked to viral infections such as enteroviruses, adenoviruses, and the mosquito-borne West Nile virus.\n\n**Symptoms and Diagnosis**\n\nSymptoms of AFM include pain in the arms and legs, an inability to pass urine, and respiratory failure. The condition can be diagnosed through a combination of clinical evaluation, magnetic resonance imaging (MRI), and laboratory tests. However, there is no specific treatment for AFM, and most cases are treated with physical therapy and supportive care.\n\n**Outbreaks and Cases**\n\nThe CDC reported 50 cases of AFM across 24 states from January 1 to August 31, 2016, compared to 21 cases in 2015. In 2014, there were 120 cases reported between August and December, all of which were children. The average age of children affected by AFM is 7, and most have had to be placed on respirators.\n\n**Investigation and Prevention**\n\nThe CDC is investigating the current outbreak of AFM, and health officials are advising people to wash their hands regularly, avoid close contact with sick people, and clean surfaces with a disinfectant. There are no vaccines available for the strain of enterovirus linked to AFM, but good hygiene practices can help prevent the spread of the virus.\n\n**Person